From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 22:36:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10971/11171] Record the first post-fix induce: truncation
 gone, induction not unlocked

The live r11l run wrote an engine at 02:05:36Z, 170 lines. Before the n_ctx fix
the induce step failed with missing ('engine',) and TRUNCATED BY SHARED CONTEXT
POOL at chars_final 0, so the fix did what it was for: the model finishes
writing, and decode runs 33.8 to 38.6 tok/s against 8 pre-fix.

What it wrote is a memorized delta table. The engine opens with a tuple of
literal per-transition delta strings transcribed from the rows it was shown, not
a rule that generates them. That is the memorization class the 2026-08-27
fine-read measured as the largest failure mode, at 100% prefix against 50 to 75%
held-out, and it measured that at roughly 6.6k-token prompts where there was no
context pressure at all.

The two compose into the uncomfortable reading: raising the window fixed a real
defect and moved the bottleneck not at all, because the bottleneck was never the
wall. It is what the model writes once it has room to finish.

Recorded with its limits rather than as a result. One engine, one game, one run
still in progress, and the engine is unscored -- exact_acc and cell_recall
against held-out transitions are what would make it rigorous, and
arc_e3_induced_model_quality.py can produce them offline once the run lands.

Also records that the run touched no tracked evidence and that both GPU
processes are its own, since milestone 609 has no ARC task and misattribution
here would be easy.

